<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Charles Brooke, Rajah of Sarawak</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1829–1917</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Charles Anthoni Johnson Brooke</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Charles Brooke was the second <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajah-of-sarawak">White Rajah</a> of Sarawak, succeeding his uncle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-brooke">James Brooke</a> in 1868 after years commanding forts and Iban forces. During a reign of nearly fifty years he greatly enlarged Sarawak at the expense of Brunei, suppressed headhunting and encouraged Chinese settlers to grow <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepper">pepper</a>. Sarawak became a British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> in 1888.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charles-brooke-rajah-of-sarawak.jpg" alt="Charles Brooke, Rajah of Sarawak" width="320" height="432" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alexander Bassano · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Charles_Brooke,_Rajah_of_Sarawak.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
